course: books
lesson: 5
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter III, part 1: The driest thing and the Caucus-race
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Alice and the animals climb out of the pool onto the bank, soaking wet, cross and uncomfortable, and argue about how to get dry; Alice even has a long squabble with a Lory who won't say how old it is.
explain: The Mouse, who seems to be in charge, makes everyone sit in a ring and reads them "the driest thing" it knows: a boring history lesson about William the Conqueror. Of course, nobody gets any drier.
explain: The Duck and the Lory keep interrupting, and when the Dodo suggests something in very long words, the Eaglet tells it to "Speak English!"
explain: The Dodo's idea is a Caucus-race: everyone runs round a rough circle, starting and stopping whenever they like, until after half an hour they are all dry.
explain: Then the Dodo thinks hard, finger on forehead, and announces that "Everybody has won, and all must have prizes": a silly, cheerful scene that pokes fun at pompous meetings and races with no rules.
fact: The party on the bank is "dripping wet, cross, and uncomfortable": birds with draggled (wet and dirty) feathers and animals with fur clinging to them.
fact: Alice soon talks with the animals "as if she had known them all her life", and argues with the Lory, who only says "I am older than you, and must know better", but refuses to tell its age, so the argument can go no further.
fact: The Mouse is "a person of authority among them", meaning someone the others obey; they all sit "in a large ring, with the Mouse in the middle", and Alice watches it anxiously, sure she will "catch a bad cold" if she does not get dry soon.
fact: The Mouse says "Ahem!" (a little cough to get attention) and calls its history lesson "the driest thing I know": "dry" means without water, and also means boring.
fact: The lesson is about William the Conqueror, the English accepting him, and Edwin and Morcar, the earls of Mercia and Northumbria; earls were powerful lords.
fact: The Lory says "Ugh!", then pretends it did not speak: "Not I!"
fact: When the Mouse says the archbishop "found it advisable" (found it a sensible idea), the Duck asks "Found what?", because when a duck finds "it", "it's generally a frog or a worm".
fact: Asked how she is getting on, Alice says "As wet as ever", "it doesn't seem to dry me at all".
fact: The Dodo says "I move that the meeting adjourn, for the immediate adoption of more energetic remedies", which means: let's stop this meeting and try something more lively straight away.
fact: The Eaglet says "Speak English! I don't know the meaning of half those long words, and, what's more, I don't believe you do either!", and some birds titter (giggle).
fact: Alice asks "What is a Caucus-race?", "not that she wanted much to know", but because the Dodo paused as if somebody ought to speak; the Dodo answers "the best way to explain it is to do it".
fact: A caucus is a meeting of members of a political party; Carroll's Caucus-race is a joke about people running round in circles and getting nowhere.
fact: The course is "a sort of circle" ("the exact shape doesn't matter"), there is no "One, two, three, and away", and everyone runs and stops when they like.
fact: After "half an hour or so" they are dry, and the Dodo calls "The race is over!"; everyone asks "But who has won?"
fact: The Dodo thinks with "one finger pressed upon its forehead", like the pictures of Shakespeare, and says "Everybody has won, and all must have prizes."
fact: Old words in today's English: "a consultation" = a talk to decide something, "assembled" = gathered, "I proceed" = I'll carry on, "inclined" = willing.
exercise: Where did Alice and the animals gather after the pool? | On the bank | In the hall | In the garden | Up a tree
exercise: How did the party on the bank feel? | Wet, cross and uncomfortable | Warm, cheerful and well rested | Hungry, sleepy and a bit bored | Proud, happy and ready to race
exercise: What did they need to do first? | Get dry again | Find the key | Eat some dinner | Catch the Rabbit
exercise: Who did Alice have a long argument with? | The Lory | The Duck | The Dodo | The Eaglet
exercise: What did the Lory keep saying in the argument? | "I am older than you, and must know better" | "Speak English! I don't know those long words" | "Everybody has won, and all must have prizes" | "Found it, of course you know what it means"
exercise: Why could the argument with the Lory go no further? | The Lory would not tell how old it was | Alice fell asleep halfway through it | The Mouse told them both to be quiet | The Lory flew off into the dark wood
exercise: Who seemed to be the person of authority among them? | The Mouse | The Lory | The Eaglet | The Duck
exercise: How did they all sit to listen to the Mouse? | In a large ring with the Mouse in the middle | In neat rows like children in a classroom | On a long bench along the edge of the pool | On top of each other in a big muddy heap
exercise: Why did Alice watch the Mouse so anxiously? | She feared she would catch a bad cold if not dry soon | She thought it was going to talk about her cat Dinah | She was waiting for it to show her the way home | She was afraid it would bite the Dodo on the nose
exercise: What did the Mouse call its history lesson? | "the driest thing I know" | "the longest tale in the land" | "a very sad story indeed" | "the funniest joke I know"
exercise: Who was the history lesson about? | William the Conqueror | The White Rabbit | Shakespeare the poet | The Duchess
exercise: Who said "Ugh!" during the lesson? | The Lory | The Duck | The Eaglet | Alice
exercise: When asked "Did you speak?", what did the Lory answer? | "Not I!" | "Yes, I did!" | "Speak English!" | "Ahem!"
exercise: According to the Duck, what is "it" usually, when a duck finds it? | A frog or a worm | A crust of bread | A shiny pebble | A fish or a snail
exercise: How did Alice say she felt after the history lesson? | As wet as ever | Nice and dry at last | A bit warmer | Very sleepy
exercise: Who suggested more "energetic remedies"? | The Dodo | The Mouse | The Eaglet | The Lory
exercise: What did the Eaglet shout at the Dodo? | "Speak English!" | "Sit down, all of you!" | "Not I!" | "Ugh!"
exercise: What did the Eaglet think about the Dodo and its long words? | It did not believe the Dodo knew what they meant | It thought the Dodo was the cleverest bird there | It wanted the Dodo to use even longer words | It was sure the Dodo had learnt them at school
exercise: Here "tittered" means... | Giggled | Shivered | Shouted | Whistled
exercise: What did the Dodo say a Caucus-race was best explained by? | Doing it | A book | A drawing in the sand | A song
exercise: What shape was the race-course? | A sort of circle, though the shape did not matter | A perfectly straight line from one tree to another | A square marked out with sticks at each corner | A zigzag up and down the bank and back to the pool
exercise: How did the Caucus-race start? | There was no start; they began when they liked | The Dodo shouted "One, two, three, and away!" | Alice blew the Rabbit's whistle very loudly | The Mouse dropped a leaf to start them off
exercise: About how long did they run? | Half an hour or so | Five minutes | All day long | Two whole hours and more
exercise: How did the Dodo sit while thinking who had won? | With one finger pressed on its forehead | With both wings folded behind its back | With its head tucked under one wing | With its eyes tightly shut and humming
exercise: What did the Dodo decide in the end? | Everybody has won, and all must have prizes | Alice has won, because she is the biggest | Nobody has won, because there was no start | The Mouse has won, because it is in charge
exercise: Here "a consultation" means... | A talk to decide something | A visit to the doctor | A long swim in the sea | A race round a circle
exercise: Thinking back: where did all these wet creatures come from? | They had fallen into the pool of Alice's tears | They had been caught out in a heavy rainstorm | They had been swimming in the sea at the seaside | They had been washed by the White Rabbit's maid
exercise: Thinking back: what had the Mouse promised to tell on the shore? | Its history, and why it hates cats and dogs | The way to the lovely garden through the door | Who had won the race long ago in the pool | How to make a daisy-chain without daisies
test: Noah goes: "The Mouse told them a scary story to warm them up." Put him right. | It read a boring history lesson to make them "dry" | It told a ghost story to make them shiver | It sang a song so they would dance and dry | It told them the way to a warm fire nearby
test: Priya, millennial: "The Mouse's lesson was lowkey the most mid thing ever." ("mid" = boring, not good.) Which word does the Mouse itself use for it? | Driest | Saddest | Longest | Funniest
test: Why is the Mouse's plan to dry them with history a joke? | "Dry" can mean boring as well as not wet | History books soak up water like a towel | Mice always dry themselves by reading | The Mouse was trying to make them sleep
test: What would have happened if they had kept listening to the Mouse's history? | They would have stayed just as wet | They would have dried off in a minute | They would have fallen into the pool | They would have caught the Rabbit
test: Mason from Texas: "Y'all, that Duck's dumb, he don't know what 'it' means." ("y'all" = you all.) What was the Duck really asking? | What exactly the archbishop found | What the word "it" means in English | Where the frogs and worms had gone | Why the Mouse was so cross with him
test: True or false: the Caucus-race had a clear start and a clear finish line. | False: they started when they liked, and the Dodo just stopped it | True: the Dodo said "One, two, three, and away" and drew a line | True: they ran to a big tree and back again to finish | False: the race never started because everyone was too wet
test: Aunt Kath, rough: "That Dodo's a pompous old windbag, all long words and no sense." ("windbag" = someone who talks a lot and says little.) Which of its lines shows this best? | "I move that the meeting adjourn" | "The race is over!" | "Hand it over here" | "But who has won?"
test: Why did Alice ask "What is a Caucus-race?"? | The Dodo paused as if somebody ought to speak | She really wanted to enter and win the race | The Mouse had told her she must ask questions | She had heard of one at school in a lesson
test: Did the Caucus-race work? | Yes: after half an hour they were all dry | No: they were all still as wet as ever | Yes: Alice won it and got a gold medal | No: the Dodo stopped it after one step
test: Why is "Everybody has won" a funny answer? | In a real race there is a winner, but this one had no rules | Everybody crossed the finish line at exactly the same time | The Dodo was the only one fast enough to finish the race | Nobody really ran, so everybody was given the same score
test: Looking back: the Mouse reads about William the Conqueror. What did Alice once guess about the Mouse and William? | That it came over with him as a French mouse | That it had once been his pet mouse at court | That it had read a book that he had written | That it was frightened of him and his soldiers
test: Looking back: Alice has been nine feet tall and a few inches tall. How does she seem now, chatting on the bank with the animals? | Small enough to argue with a Lory as an equal | Still nine feet tall and towering over everyone | Ten inches high and standing on the glass table | Exactly the size of the White Rabbit's little house
test: How has Alice changed since the hall, where she cried alone? | She is now in company, talking "as if she had known them all her life" | She is now much bigger than the others and orders them about | She is now asleep and dreaming of her sister on the bank again | She is now angry with everyone and refuses to say a word to them
test: Kirsty from Aberdeen: "The craturs were drookit, ken." ("craturs" = creatures, "drookit" = soaked, "ken" = you know.) What does this say about the animals? | They were soaking wet | They were very hungry | They were fast asleep | They were very cross
test: ★★★ The Lory says "I am older than you, and must know better". Why is that not a good reason? | Being older does not prove you are right | The Lory was really much younger than Alice | Only birds that are old can ever be wrong | Alice was in fact older than every bird there
test: ★★★ Read carefully: did Alice ask "What is a Caucus-race?" because she wanted to know? | No: "not that she wanted much to know" | Yes: she was eager to learn the rules | Yes: she wanted to run and win a prize | No: the Mouse asked it instead of her
test: ★★★ The party ran "half an hour or so". If they started at a quarter past four, about when did the Dodo shout "The race is over!"? | About a quarter to five | About half past four | About five o'clock | About a quarter past five
exam: Identify the character who appeared to exercise authority over the assembled party upon the bank. | The Mouse | The Dodo | The Lory | The Eaglet
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the protagonist's argument with the Lory could not be concluded. | The Lory declined to disclose its age | The Lory departed for its residence | The Mouse forbade further conversation | The protagonist withdrew in tears
exam: It is given that the Mouse recited a passage of history with the stated object of drying the company. Determine the outcome as reported by the protagonist. | She remained as wet as she had been previously | She was rendered entirely dry within a minute | She was rendered drier on one side than the other | She was caused to fall asleep with no further effect
exam: Select the response most accurately characterising the Dodo's determination upon the conclusion of the race. | That every participant had won and must receive a prize | That the protagonist alone had won by virtue of her size | That the race was void, there having been no start | That the Mouse had won by reason of its authority
